use std
into noise.amni

Namespace noise; imports math and vec. Seeded coherent noise in the spirit of FastNoise2, all in `real` with `double3` points: value, Perlin (gradient), simplex and cellular (Worley) base noise, fBm, ridged and billow fractals, domain warp, and fills of 2D and 3D grids. Seeds are u64 and all u64 math wraps. Kind constants: `VALUE = 0`, `PERLIN = 1`, `SIMPLEX = 2`, `CELL_F1 = 3`, `CELL_F2 = 4`, `CELL_EDGE = 5` (F2 minus F1) and `CELL_VALUE = 6`. Fractal constants: `NONE = 0`, `FBM = 1`, `RIDGED = 2` and `BILLOW = 3`. `GOLDEN64 u64 = 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15`.

`mix64(v u64) u64` is the splitmix64 finalizer: z = v; z = (z XOR z shifted right 30) times `0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9`; z = (z XOR z shifted right 27) times `0x94D049BB133111EB`; return z XOR z shifted right 31. `hash3(seed u64, ix int, iy int, iz int) u64`: h = seed, then for ix, iy and iz in that order h = mix64(h plus the coordinate (as u64, wrapping) times GOLDEN64). `unit_of(h u64) real` is (h shifted right 11) as real divided by 9007199254740992.0, in [0, 1); `signed_of(h u64) real` is 2 times `unit_of(h)` minus 1. `fade(t real) real` is t*t*t*(t*(t*6-15)+10).

`grad_dot(h u64, x real, y real, z real) real` picks one of the twelve edge gradients: g = h modulo 12 (as int); first is x when g is below 8, else y; second is y when g is below 4, else z; a is first when g is even, else 0 minus first; b is second when (g integer-divided by 2) is even, else 0 minus second; the result is a plus b.

`perlin3(seed u64, p double3) real` is improved Perlin gradient noise, written exactly like this so other code can rely on its bits: gx, gy and gz are the floors of p's components, ix, iy and iz those as int, x = p.x minus gx (likewise y and z), u, v and w their `fade`s. c000 = `grad_dot(hash3(seed, ix, iy, iz), x, y, z)`, c100 the same at ix + 1 with x minus 1.0, c010 at iy + 1 with y minus 1.0, c110 at ix + 1 and iy + 1 with x minus 1.0 and y minus 1.0, and c001, c101, c011 and c111 the same four with iz + 1 and z minus 1.0. near = lerp(lerp(c000, c100, u), lerp(c010, c110, u), v); far = lerp(lerp(c001, c101, u), lerp(c011, c111, u), v); the result is lerp(near, far, w), not clamped.

`value3(seed u64, p double3) real` is value noise: the same lattice cell, fraction and fades as `perlin3`, each corner's value `signed_of(hash3(seed, corner))`, blended with the same lerp order; in [-1, 1].

`simplex3(seed u64, p double3) real` is 3D simplex noise (Gustavson): with F3 = 1/3 and G3 = 1/6, s = (p.x + p.y + p.z) times F3, i, j, k = the floors of p.x + s, p.y + s, p.z + s; t = (i + j + k) times G3; x0 = p.x minus (i minus t), likewise y0 and z0. The second and third corner offsets (i1, j1, k1) and (i2, j2, k2) follow the usual ordering of x0, y0 and z0: when x0 is at least y0: if y0 is at least z0 they are (1,0,0) and (1,1,0); else if x0 is at least z0 (1,0,0) and (1,0,1); else (0,0,1) and (1,0,1); otherwise (x0 below y0): if y0 is below z0 (0,0,1) and (0,1,1); else if x0 is below z0 (0,1,0) and (0,1,1); else (0,1,0) and (1,1,0). Corner offsets: x1 = x0 minus i1 plus G3, x2 = x0 minus i2 plus 2 G3, x3 = x0 minus 1 plus 3 G3 (likewise y and z). Each of the four corners adds, when q = 0.5 minus its squared distance is above 0, q to the fourth power times `grad_dot(hash3(seed, cell of that corner), its offset)`, the cells being (i, j, k), (i + i1, j + j1, k + k1), (i + i2, j + j2, k + k2) and (i + 1, j + 1, k + 1). The result is 76.88 times the sum, clamped to [-1, 1].

`cell_point(seed u64, cx int, cy int, cz int) double3, u64`: h = hash3(seed, cx, cy, cz); the feature point of that cell is (cx + unit_of(h), cy + unit_of(mix64(h plus GOLDEN64)), cz + unit_of(mix64(h plus 2 times GOLDEN64))) (full jitter: anywhere in the cell); h is returned too.

`cellular3(seed u64, p double3, kind int) real`: the cell of p is (floor of each component) as ints. Search the 27 cells with offsets -1 to 1 on each axis, keeping the smallest squared distance d1 with its cell's hash and the second smallest d2 (both start at 1e30; a closer point pushes d1 down to d2). Then, so the answer is exact (with full jitter F2 is never above the square root of 3, so cells two away are the farthest that can matter), visit the 98 cells with offsets -2 to 2 on every axis that are not in the inner 27: for each axis the gap from p to that cell is 0 for offset 0, the fraction of p for offset -1, 1 minus the fraction for offset +1, 1 plus the fraction for -2 and 2 minus the fraction for +2; skip the cell when the sum of the squared gaps is at least d2; otherwise test its point the same way. f1 = square root of d1, f2 = square root of d2. CELL_F1 gives 2 f1 minus 1; CELL_F2 gives f2 minus 1; CELL_EDGE gives 2 (f2 minus f1) minus 1; CELL_VALUE gives `signed_of(mix64(nearest cell's hash plus 3 times GOLDEN64))`. Every result is clamped to [-1, 1].

`base(kind int, seed u64, p double3) real` dispatches: VALUE `value3`, PERLIN `perlin3`, SIMPLEX `simplex3`, the four cellular kinds `cellular3`; any other kind gives 0.

A `Noise` struct holds the settings: kind int, seed u64, step u64 (added to the seed each octave), frequency real, fractal int, octaves int, lacunarity real, gain real, warp real (domain warp amount) and warp_freq real. `make(kind int, seed int) Noise` gives that kind with seed as u64 (wrapping), step 1, frequency 1, fractal NONE, octaves 3, lacunarity 2, gain 0.5, warp 0 and warp_freq 1.

`layered(n Noise, p double3) real` is the raw fractal average, written exactly like this so other code can rely on its bits: with fractal NONE, `base(n.kind, n.seed, p times n.frequency)`. Otherwise sum = 0, total = 0, amp = 1, freq = n.frequency and s = n.seed; for each of n.octaves octaves: b = `base(n.kind, s, p times freq)`; FBM adds amp times b to sum, RIDGED adds amp times ((1 minus the absolute value of b) squared, written r times r), BILLOW adds amp times (2 times the absolute value of b minus 1); then total goes up by amp, amp is multiplied by n.gain, freq by n.lacunarity, and s becomes s plus n.step. The result is sum divided by total (0 when total is 0).

`warp_point(n Noise, p double3) double3` gives p unchanged when n.warp is 0; otherwise with q = p times n.warp_freq, p plus n.warp times (`perlin3(n.seed plus 101, q)`, `perlin3(n.seed plus 102, q)`, `perlin3(n.seed plus 103, q)`).

`sample(n Noise, p double3) real` is `layered(n, warp_point(n, p))`, with RIDGED mapped by 2 r minus 1, clamped to [-1, 1].

`fill2(n Noise, x0 real, z0 real, y real, step real, w int, h int, out)` appends w times h f32 values to the growable f32 list out, row by row (j from 0 to h - 1 outer, i inner): `sample(n, (x0 + i times step, y, z0 + j times step))`. `fill3(n Noise, origin double3, step real, w int, h int, d int, out)` appends w times h times d values, k outer, then j, then i inner, at origin plus (i, j, k) times step.
